es. The architecture distributes instances across 2 Availability Zones, meeting requirement N2 for high availability. Auto Scaling responds to CloudWatch CPU alarms, launching additional instances within minutes to handle traffic spikes.\n\n**Performance Efficiency**: Upgrading from t3.micro (40 req/s) to t3.medium (160 req/s) provides 4x capacity per instance, reducing the instance count needed and improving cost efficiency. ElastiCache reduces database query latency from 50-100ms to 1-5ms for cached content, improving user experience and reducing RDS CPU utilization. The db.t3.medium RDS instance (2 vCPUs, 4 GB RAM vs. db.t3.micro 2 vCPUs, 1 GB RAM) handles increased connection pools and query complexity without throttling. CloudFront's 50% cache hit ratio offloads half the traffic from the origin, and the increased data transfer allocation (2,000 GB vs. 200 GB) supports 10x growth. Auto Scaling enables right-sizing: running 2 instances during normal hours and scaling to 4 only during the 60-hour monthly peak optimizes resource utilization.\n\n**Cost Optimization**: Total monthly cost is $645.89, within the $750 budget (86% utilization). ElastiCache ($99.28) eliminates the need for sticky sessions at the ALB, enabling better load distribution and reducing over-provisioning. The larger instance type (t3.medium) costs more per hour ($0.0416 vs. $0.0104) but provides better price-performance: $0.26 per 1000 req/s vs. $0.26 for t3.micro, with fewer instances needed overall. RDS Multi-AZ ($99.28) costs double a single-AZ instance but eliminates the need for a separate read replica for high availability, reducing operational complexity. The architecture scales horizontally during the 60-hour peak (8% of the month) rather than over-provisioning for peak 24/7, saving approximately 50% on compute costs compared to running 4 instances continuously. Reserved Instances or Savings Plans could reduce costs by 30-40% for the baseline 2-instance deployment in a production commitment.",
