ce Efficiency: ElastiCache dramatically improves performance by caching database query results and session data, reducing database load by 60-80% and cutting response latency from 200ms to under 50ms for cached content. T3.medium instances with burstable CPU handle variable load efficiently, accumulating credits during off-peak hours (670 hours at 150 rps) and consuming them during peak periods (60 hours at 750 rps). CloudFront's 50% cache hit rate reduces origin load and improves global user experience. Right-sizing compute to match actual demand (t3.medium vs. over-provisioning to m6g.large) optimizes cost-performance. The ALB distributes traffic evenly across instances and AZs, preventing hotspots.\n\nCost Optimization: At $745.87 monthly, the proposal stays within the $750 budget (N5) while delivering 4x the capacity. Key optimizations include using T3 burstable instances instead of fixed-performance types (saves 46% versus m6g.large equivalents); maintaining db.t3.micro rather than over-provisioning to db.t3.medium (saves $224/month); single NAT gateway instead of one per AZ (saves $32.85/month); ElastiCache eliminates the need for database read replicas that would cost $248/month; CloudFront caching reduces origin compute hours and NAT gateway data transfer. The architecture scales spend with demand—60 hours of peak capacity costs only $5 in additional compute versus continuous over-provisioning.\n\nSustainability: The proposed architecture minimizes resource waste through right-sizing and dynamic scaling. During off-peak periods (92% of the month), only 2 t3.medium instances run, reducing energy consumption by 50% versus always running 4 instances. ElastiCache reduces database CPU utilization by caching frequent queries, lowering energy per transaction. T3 instances use AWS Nitro System for improved performance-per-watt. CloudFront edge caching reduces data transfer distances and origin compute cycles.",
